Q1: In 魏文侯’s time, 西门豹 was appointed as the magistrate of 邺. He learned that the local elders and officials were collecting money from villagers to “marry” a girl to the 河伯 (river god), causing poverty and fear. 西门豹 exposed the fraud, publicly humiliated and drowned the 巫 and officials, and then used wit and persuasion to end the practice. He also dug twelve canals to irrigate farmland, bringing lasting prosperity to the people.

Q2: The victims were usually young, attractive-looking girls/women from local families, especially those whose families had “good” daughters.

Q3: The old practice benefited the local 三老、廷掾、巫祝 and other officials/elders, who collected money and shared the proceeds.

Q4: The protagonist used wits more than brute force. He first exposed the deception by publicly inspecting the “河伯妇” and mocking her, then used theatrical threats and humiliation to force the 巫 and officials to “report” to the river god. He also used persuasion and public spectacle to end the practice. Force appears in the form of drowning people in the river, but it is framed as a dramatic, witty punishment rather than a purely violent act.

Q5: Views differ. From a justice/reform perspective, it can be seen as morally justified: the old practice exploited the poor and caused fear, so exposing and ending it served the people. From a legal/moral perspective, drowning the 巫 and officials was excessive and unlawful, so it is not fully moral. From a political/social perspective, it may be seen as effective but harsh; it restored order and prosperity, yet it also shows the limits of coercion. Overall, the reform is praiseworthy, but the violent punishment is ethically questionable.

Q6: The passage compares three rulers: 子產, 子賤, and 西门豹. It says that under 子產 the people could not deceive him, under 子賤 the people could not bear to deceive him, and under 西门豹 the people dared not deceive him. The question asks which of the three was most virtuous, and says that those who govern well should be able to distinguish them.

The comment is fair in the sense that it invites comparison of different styles of governance: 子產 represents trust/knowledge, 子賤 represents moral restraint, and 西门豹 represents fear/authority. But it is not fully fair if it treats “not daring to deceive” as the highest virtue, because fear-based rule is not necessarily the best form of governance. A more balanced view is that 西门豹 was effective and reform-minded, but 子產 and 子賤 may represent more humane or moral forms of rule. [end of text]


